{"step_index":724,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T14:48:44Z","thinking":"Need to create a playbook to check trp-wextp-be servers, starting with kdbx-pool checks. The playbook must then examine front-end server connections to the back-end servers. Finally, back-end servers require checks for PHP version, ProxySQL configuration and status, and the root directory for each domain.\n\n\n`playbooks/audit-wextp-cluster.yml` will be the name of the new playbook. The first step involves identifying back-end servers, initially considering a `kdbx-pool.yml` approach, but a manual configuration for front-end server groups will be necessary as well. A dedicated play will dynamically create `wextp_backends` and `wextp_frontends` Ansible groups, removing the need for a complex inventory file. The back-ends are added dynamically to avoid the user having to maintain the ip addresses and usernames.\n\n\nFront-end server analysis requires inspecting Nginx configuration files to map domains to back-end servers, resolving upstream definitions. This necessitates a custom data structure capturing domain, front-end details, upstream name, and target back-ends. The results of this analysis will be stored as facts for later use.\n\nBack-end server checks will cover PHP version identification, ProxySQL configuration and status, and the determination of domain root directories. This inspection will be performed using a non-destructive method to gather the necessary data.\n\n\nPHP version, FPM service status, and listening ports will be checked on each back-end. ProxySQL's status, version, configuration, and listening ports are targeted for collection. Finally, root directories for each domain on the back-end will be examined, including their attributes and relevant files. A structured JSON output named `be_audit_results` will store the consolidated results.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"ssh -o BatchMode=y