{"step_index":744,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T14:58:07Z","thinking":"Initial testing confirms the `import_playbook` with the specified search variable successfully resolves `trp-wextp-be` hosts.\n\nNow, considering dynamic inventory population, the goal is to register all `trp-wextp-be*` and `trp-wextp-fe*` hosts using a specific method within the Ansible workflow. This will involve the use of a host-adding mechanism, likely on the `localhost` during a subsequent play.\n\n\nThe playbook will load a server pool, decode the data, and then filter to register backend and frontend hosts. Backend hosts matching the pattern `trp-wextp-be*` and the `WEXTP` group will be added to the `wextp_backends` group. The process will repeat to add frontend hosts to `wextp_frontends`.\n\n\nConsidering existing inventory usage, host registration should handle potential updates to existing groups. To ensure local CLI tools function correctly, discovered frontend and backend hosts need to be written into the inventory file.\n\nThe next stage will involve examining frontend server configurations. A Python script will scan Nginx configuration files to map upstreams and resolve backend targets for each server block.\n\n\nThe frontend configuration analysis will focus on resolving backend targets by parsing Nginx configuration and handling upstream definitions or direct host-port specifications. This process involves extracting backend server details and compiling domain route records.\n\nSubsequently, a backend check will be performed to determine the PHP version, ProxySQL configuration and status, and root directory for each domain on each backend server. This will use an embedded Python script to determine the PHP version via `php -v`, scan for all installed PHP CLI versions, and identify active PHP-FPM services.\n\n\nPHP-FPM will be examined to identify active pools and their corresponding ports by parsing configuration files and examining listening sockets. ProxySQL's status, version, configuration, and listening ports will be determined via command-line tools and configuration file analysis. Finally, a scan of the root directories will identify domains, along with their ownership, permissions, and index files.\n\n\nBackend server auditing focuses on verifying the existence of expected webroots for each domain by checking `/var/www/`.  This will involve extracting the branch name to compare the existence of backend server webroots.\n\nThe next step generates a detailed report for each backend server, including PHP, ProxySQL configuration summaries, and a listing of hosted webroots, along with a frontend-to-backend routing topology table.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"ssh -o BatchMode=yes -p 30 koby-bowser-ansible@172.30.230.97 'python3 -c \\\"\\nimport glob, re, json\\n\\nupstreams = {}\\n# Parse upstream definitions\\nfor conf_path in glob.glob(\\\\\\\"/etc/nginx/omt/*.conf\\\\\\\") + glob.glob(\\\\\\\"/etc/nginx/conf.d/*.conf\\\\\\\") + glob.glob(\\\\\\\"/etc/nginx/*.conf\\\\\\\"):\\n    try:\\n        with open(conf_path) as f:\\n            content = f.read()\\n        for m in re.finditer(r\\\\\\\"upstream\\\\s+([a-zA-Z0-9_\\\\-]+)\\\\s*\\\\{([^}]+)\\\\}\\\\\\\", content):\\n            up_name = m.group(1)\\n            body = m.group(2)\\n            servers = []\\n            for line in body.split(\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\\\\"):\\n                line = line.strip()\\n                if line.startswith(\\\\\\\"server \\\\\\\") and not line.startswith(\\\\\\\"#\\\\\\\"):\\n                    parts = line.replace(\\\\\\\";\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\").split()\\n                    if len(parts) >= 2:\\n                        target = parts[1]\\n                        host = target.split(\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\")[0]\\n                        port = target.split(\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\")[1] if \\\\\\\":\\\\\\\" in target else \\\\\\\"80\\\\\\\"\\n                        s