{"step_index":767,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T15:05:40Z","thinking":"All hosts successfully responded, indicating a fully functional passwordless connection across the cluster. The next step involves creating a playbook for auditing the WExtP topology, considering whether the filename should include \"topology\" or \"cluster\".\n\n\nThe initial phase retrieves backend server information, dynamically populating the inventory. The front-end audit will then analyze Nginx configuration files to map domains to back-end routes using an embedded parser. The back-end audit will gather PHP version information, examine PHP-FPM service states and listening ports and gather domain webroot information.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"---\\n# ==============================================================================\\n# Playbook: Audit WEXTP Cluster — Frontend/Backend Topology & BE Deep Dive\\n# ==============================================================================\\n# 1. Phase 1: Queries KeePass server pool via playbooks/kdbx-pool.yml for all\\n#             trp-wextp-be nodes and dynamically registers inventory groups.\\n# 2. Phase 2: Audits Front-Ends (fe1, fe2, fe3) to discover which Backend nodes\\n#             (and ports) each hosted domain connects to via fastcgi_pass/proxy_pass.\\n# 3. Phase 3: Audits Back-Ends (be1 to be11) for:\\n#             - PHP CLI version, active PHP-FPM services & listening pool ports\\n#             - ProxySQL service status, version, config & listening ports\\n#             - Root directories (/var/www/*) and match against routed domains.\\n# 4. Phase 4: Consolidates findings into aligned cross-cluster topology tables.\\n#\\n# Usage:\\n#   ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.ini playbooks/audit-wextp-topology.yml\\n# ==============================================================================\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Phase 1: Query Server Pool for Backends via kdbx-pool\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n- name: \\\"Phase 1a - Query Server Pool for Backends via kdbx-pool\\\"\\n  ansible.builtin.import_playbook: kdbx-pool.yml\\n  vars:\\n    search: \\\"trp-wextp-be\\\"\\n\\n- name: \\\"Phase 1b - Ensure WEXTP Dynamic Inventory Groups from Pool\\\"\\n  hosts: localhost\\n  connection: local\\n  gather_facts: false\\n  tasks:\\n    - name: Load server pool cache\\n      ansible.builtin.slurp:\\n        src: \\\"{{ playbook_dir }}/../inventory/server_pool.json\\\"\\n      register: _pool_raw\\n      failed_when: false\\n\\n    - name: Dynamically register backend hosts if not present\\n      ansible.builtin.add_host:\\n        name: \\\"{{ item.title.split()[0] }}\\\"\\n        ansible_host: \\\"{{ item.primary_ip | default(item.ips[0]) }}\\\"\n<truncated 20108 bytes>","Description":"\"Add audit-wextp-topology playbook to analyze FE-to-BE routing, PHP versions, ProxySQL and webroots\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/home/koby/LINUX/playbooks/audit-wextp-topology.yml\"","toolAction":"\"Writing audit-wextp-topology playbook\"","toolSummary":"\"Write topology playbook\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
